# ⛔ AN ALLOWLIST, NOT A DENYLIST, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT.
#
# There was no .dockerignore at all, so every build shipped a 1.2 GB context: 159 MB of
# node_modules the image reinstalls from the lockfile anyway, 39 MB of .git, the private
# planning/ notes, the gitignored corpora — and backups/, which holds full dumps of a real
# client's product truth. A denylist would have had to name each of those, and the next large
# directory somebody adds would walk straight back in.
#
# So: ignore everything, then name the six things the Dockerfile actually COPYs. A new file is
# excluded until somebody says otherwise, which is the right default for a build context that has
# had customer data sitting in it.
*

!package.json
!package-lock.json
!tsconfig.json
!src
!bin
# ⛔ The migrations are part of the image. `migrationsDir()` walks up from dist/ to find them, and
#    an instance that cannot is indistinguishable from one looking at an empty database.
!drizzle
